const BOUND_PATHWAY = "{\"id\":\"marijuana-record-destruction-under-the-mrta\",\"label\":\"Marijuana-record destruction under the MRTA\",\"summary\":\"Convictions for unlawful possession and 5th-degree possession of marijuana are expunged/destroyed automatically. An optional request to OCA can confirm destruction. These do NOT count toward the 160.59 two-conviction limit. Proposed Order (petition pathway)\",\"sourceRef\":\"section:415-435; section:68-109; pathways:68-109; corpus-unit:22; section:233-260; section:314-344; corpus-unit:4; corpus-unit:8\",\"sourceEvidenceRefs\":[\"section:415-435\",\"section:68-109\",\"pathways:68-109\",\"corpus-unit:22\",\"section:233-260\",\"section:314-344\",\"corpus-unit:4\",\"corpus-unit:8\",\"corpus-unit:9\",\"corpus-unit:56\",\"corpus-unit:85\",\"corpus-unit:110\",\"corpus-unit:1\",\"corpus-unit:88\"],\"ruleClauses\":[\"Convictions for unlawful possession and 5th-degree possession\",\"of marijuana are expunged/destroyed automatically. An optional\",\"request to OCA can confirm destruction. These do NOT count\",\"toward the 160.59 two-conviction limit.\",\"Proposed Order (petition pathway)\",\"A 160.59 application is decided by court order. If granted, the order directs DCJS and the courts to seal the\",\"specified conviction(s) from public and most private access, while preserving access for law enforcement,\",\"New York Record Sealing & Clean Slate — Agent Training Reference ·13\",\"prosecutors, courts, licensing for vulnerable-population and police/peace-officer roles, and firearms licensing,\",\"This reference is an internal training and retrieval aid for the Wilma assistant. It summarizes New York statutes and common\",\"2024, with implementation through Nov 2027; the agent must verify the current text of CPL §§ 160.50–160.59, the\",\"eligible-offense and Clean Slate exclusion lists, DCJS Record Review procedures, and Clean Slate implementation status before\",\"New York Record Sealing & Clean Slate — Agent Training Reference ·14\",\"Five mechanisms, considered in routing order.\",\"Non-conviction sealing (CPL 160.50 / 160.55)\",\"When a case ends in the defendant's favor — dismissal, acquittal, declined prosecution, an adjournment in\",\"contemplation of dismissal (ACD) that is dismissed, or a vacated/overturned conviction — the record is\",\"SEALED automatically under CPL 160.50. Where the case ends in a non-criminal VIOLATION or traffic\",\"infraction, CPL 160.55 provides a more limited sealing. No application is needed; the court directs sealing at\",\"Marijuana destruction / expungement (MRTA)\",\"The one true erasure in New York. Convictions for unlawful possession of marijuana and fifth-degree criminal\",\"possession of marijuana are expunged/destroyed automatically under the MRTA. A person may also request\",\"destruction of those records through OCA. Expunged marijuana convictions do NOT count toward the\",\"two-conviction limit under CPL 160.59.\",\"Conditional / treatment sealing (CPL 160.58)\",\"A person convicted of certain drug or specified offenses who successfully completes a judicial-diversion or\",\"court-approved substance-abuse treatment program, and all other sentence terms, may have those convictions\",\"conditionally sealed. The sealing can be reopened on a subsequent arrest, and resealed if that arrest does not\"],\"triggerFields\":[\"case_outcome\",\"offense_category\",\"offense_level\"],\"caseOutcomes\":[\"convicted_other\",\"arrest_no_charge\",\"dismissed\",\"acquitted\",\"diversion_or_deferred\"],\"automatic\":true,\"filingRequired\":false,\"routeType\":\"automatic\",\"suggestedResultCode\":\"guidance_only\",\"waitingRules\":[\"An eligible misdemeanor conviction seals automatically 3 years\",\"no more than ONE of which is a felony, after at least 10 years have passed since sentencing or release\",\"Clean Slate misdemeanor Seal (auto) 3 years From sentencing/release;\",\"Clean Slate felony (160.57) Seal (auto) 8 years From sentencing/release;\",\"years misdemeanor / 8 years felony) but limited to its eligible set and still phasing in. The CPL 160.59 petition\",\"wait for the automatic system. For Clean Slate, any new conviction or pending charge during the waiting period\",\"Mechanism / matter Remedy Waiting period Notes Non-conviction (160.50) Seal None Sealed at favorable disposition Non-criminal violation Seal None Limited sealing at disposition (160.55) Marijuana possession (MRTA) Destroy/ None Automatic; OCA destruction on request expunge Drug conviction + treatment Conditional On program Reopens on new arrest (160.58) seal completion Clean Slate misdemeanor Seal (auto) 3 years From sentencing/release; (160.57) no pending charges Clean Slate felony (160.57) Seal (auto) 8 years From sentencing/release; no pending charges Discretionary petition Seal 10 years ≤ 2 convictions, ≤ 1 felony; (160.59) eligible offense The two conviction-sealing routes differ sharply in timing and effort.\",\"Clean Slate is automatic and faster (3 years misdemeanor / 8 years felony) but limited to its eligible set and still phasing in.\",\"For Clean Slate, any new conviction or pending charge during the waiting period restarts or blocks the clock.\"],\"exclusionRules\":[\"Non-drug Class A felonies — including murder and other top-tier felonies. (Drug-related Class A felonies under Penal Law Article 220 CAN still qualify.)\",\"Cases where the person received a life sentence.\",\"Any person with pending criminal charges — ineligible while a charge is open.\",\"Federal convictions and out-of-state convictions — Clean Slate reaches NY State convictions only.\",\"Two-conviction / one-felony cap (160.59). No more than two convictions, and no more than one felony, may be sealed by petition.\",\"Mixed records. If a person has both eligible and ineligible convictions, the eligible ones may still be sealed; the ineligible ones remain.\",\"Sealed records still count. A sealed conviction still counts as a prior for sentence-enhancement in any future case (160.59(10)).\",\"The 160.59 “eligible offense” definition and the Clean Slate exclusions turn on the exact Penal\",\"Law article and class of each conviction (e.g., whether a Class A felony is a drug offense under\",\"Article 220). The agent must map each conviction to its Penal Law citation and class and verify\",\"against the current statute before declaring it eligible — the offense label alone is not enough.\",\"Note also the Brooklyn DA guidance that pre-2007 PL 265.02(4) weapon convictions are not\",\"The key Clean Slate nuance is the Class A felony split: non-drug Class A felonies are permanently\",\"excluded, but Article 220 drug Class A felonies can qualify. Check the article, not just the class.\",\"Sex offenses and any offense requiring sex-offender registration. • Non-drug Class A felonies — including murder and other top-tier felonies. (Drug-related Class A felonies under Penal Law Article 220 CAN still qualify.) • Cases where the person received a life sentence. • Any person with pending criminal charges — ineligible while a charge is open. • Federal convictions and out-of-state convictions — Clean Slate reaches NY State convictions only.\",\"If a person has both eligible and ineligible convictions, the eligible ones may still be sealed; the ineligible ones remain. • Sealed records still count.\",\"AGENT GUIDANCE The key Clean Slate nuance is the Class A felony split: non-drug Class A felonies are permanently excluded, but Article 220 drug Class A felonies can qualify.\"],\"frontendBranch\":{\"entryCondition\":\"Backend matched this jurisdiction-specific route after evaluating the cited source rules.\",\"missingFactsResult\":\"needs_more_info\",\"unmetTimingResult\":\"not_yet\",\"exclusionOrAmbiguityResult\":\"likely_not_eligible\",\"matchedResult\":\"guidance_only\",\"paymentAllowed\":false}}";
const BOUND_PLAN = "{\"pathwayId\":\"marijuana-record-destruction-under-the-mrta\",\"pathwayLabel\":\"Marijuana-record destruction under the MRTA\",\"mode\":\"automatic_relief_verification_and_guidance\",\"formCandidates\":[],\"formMappingStatus\":\"not_required\",\"sourceRuleRefs\":[\"section:415-435\",\"section:68-109\",\"pathways:68-109\",\"corpus-unit:22\",\"section:233-260\",\"section:314-344\",\"corpus-unit:4\",\"corpus-unit:8\",\"corpus-unit:9\",\"corpus-unit:56\",\"corpus-unit:85\",\"corpus-unit:110\",\"corpus-unit:1\",\"corpus-unit:88\"],\"selectionRule\":\"Use only the source documents and assembly rules tied to the backend-returned jurisdiction pathway. Never substitute another state or an old MVP generator.\",\"packetReadyWhen\":[\"backend result is packet_ready or packet_ready_with_caution\",\"every source-defined pathway fact is answered\",\"all waiting and completion conditions are satisfied\",\"no exclusion, ambiguity, or required-review flag remains\",\"the selected form revision and filing location pass source-freshness checks\"],\"noPacketWhen\":[\"automatic or no-filing route\",\"needs_more_info\",\"not_yet\",\"needs_review\",\"likely_not_eligible\",\"hard_stop\"]}";
const BOUND_MEMO = "{\"trackId\":\"ny_mrta_marijuana\",\"controllingAuthority\":{\"citations\":[\"Marihuana Regulation and Taxation Act\",\"N.Y. Crim. Proc. Law § 160.50\",\"N.Y. Crim. Proc. Law § 160.50(3)(k)\",\"N.Y. Crim. Proc. Law § 160.50(5)(a)\",\"N.Y. Crim. Proc. Law § 160.50(5)(b)(i)\",\"N.Y. Crim. Proc. Law § 160.50(5)(b)(ii)\",\"N.Y. Crim. Proc. Law § 160.59\"],\"summary\":\"Convictions for the marijuana offences described in CPL § 160.50(3)(k) are vacated, dismissed and expunged by operation of law under § 160.50(5)(a). That stage is automatic: there is no waiting period, no motion, no fee and nothing for the participant to file, and under § 160.50(5)(b)(i) the chief administrator of the courts notifies the Division of Criminal Justice Services and the heads of all appropriate police departments, district attorneys' offices and other law enforcement agencies, who mark the records as expunged. Destruction is a second and distinct stage and is not automatic: § 160.50(5)(b)(i) provides that upon the written request of the individual whose case has been expunged, or their designated agent, such records shall be destroyed, and the Unified Court System publishes an application for that request through the Office of Court Administration's statewide forms library. That application is submitted to the court in which the person was convicted, one application per court of conviction; the Office of Court Administration publishes the form and does not receive it. Section 160.50(5)(b)(ii) describes what the subdivision requires as automatic vacatur, dismissal and expungement including record destruction if requested, which places the participant's request inside the same statutory mechanism rather than beside it. This is the only true erasure in New York; every other track seals. Expunged marijuana convictions do not count toward the two-conviction limit on the discretionary sealing petition, which can move a participant from ineligible to eligible there. The Marihuana Regulation and Taxation Act chapter-law citation is still not pinned and the review cites the Act generically rather than to a section.\"},\"eligibleRecordTypes\":[\"conviction\",\"court_record\"],\"eligibleDispositions\":[\"convicted\"],\"exclusions\":[\"Any marijuana offence outside the two covered categories, being unlawful possession of marijuana and fifth-degree criminal possession of marijuana.\",\"Mixed cases with non-marijuana charges.\",\"Federal and out-of-state records.\"],\"waitingPeriods\":[{\"condition\":\"Qualifying marijuana conviction\",\"duration\":\"none. The vacatur, dismissal and expungement are automatic\"},{\"condition\":\"Destruction of the expunged records\",\"duration\":\"none, but it is not automatic: it happens only on the participant's written request under CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i)\"}],\"compositionMode\":\"sequential\",\"units\":[{\"unitId\":\"ny-mrta-automatic-expungement\",\"order\":1,\"label\":\"The automatic vacatur, dismissal and expungement, and how to check it\",\"outputStrategy\":\"process_guidance\",\"outputStrategyStatus\":\"resolved\",\"packetIdentity\":\"identified\",\"description\":\"The default stage, and the one that runs first. Under CPL § 160.50(5)(a) a conviction for an offence described in § 160.50(3)(k) is vacated and dismissed and all records of it expunged, by operation of law. Nothing is filed, no motion is made, no fee is charged and the participant does nothing to obtain it: under § 160.50(5)(b)(i) the chief administrator of the courts notifies the Division of Criminal Justice Services and the heads of all appropriate police departments, district attorneys' offices and other law enforcement agencies, and those holders mark the record as expunged. No participant filing exists at this stage and none is generated. The guidance explains what expungement means, that the courts do not send a notice when it happens, how to check through a DCJS Record Review, why an absence in that report is consistent with expungement rather than proof of destruction, and the separate § 160.50(5)(b)(ii)(B) route where court records cannot be located or the expungement has not been reflected. Where the participant wants nothing beyond expungement, the route ends here.\",\"available\":true},{\"unitId\":\"ny-mrta-destruction-request\",\"order\":2,\"label\":\"Participant-signed request to the court of conviction that the expunged records be destroyed\",\"outputStrategy\":\"official_pdf_fill\",\"outputStrategyStatus\":\"resolved\",\"packetIdentity\":\"identified\",\"description\":\"The conditional stage, reached only where the participant asks for destruction. It is not a chase of a failed automatic process and does not assert that anything went wrong: destruction is simply never automatic. CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i) provides that upon the written request of the individual whose case has been expunged, or their designated agent, such records shall be destroyed, and § 160.50(5)(b)(ii) describes what the subdivision requires as automatic vacatur, dismissal and expungement including record destruction if requested. The request is therefore a conditional unit of the same statutory mechanism rather than a second remedy, and its precondition is that the case has already been expunged. The instrument is the Unified Court System's APPLICATION TO DESTROY EXPUNGED MARIHUANA CONVICTION RECORD, retained in Edition 1.2 at MRTA-DESTRUCTION-REQUEST, which the participant signs, dates and submits to the New York court in which they were convicted — a separate application to each court where they have eligible convictions — through that court's Electronic Document Delivery System, by first-class mail or in person, with no fee. This unit is a filing to a court, unlike unit 1, which has no participant document at all. What follows the submission is the court's work and not the participant's: the court acknowledges the application and forwards it to the prosecutor, the law enforcement agencies and DCJS. LegalEase prepares the participant's portion from their own answers; it does not certify eligibility, does not certify that the expungement occurred, does not advise whether to ask for destruction, does not submit the application, and does not produce a version signed by anyone but the defendant. The unit is never offered at the outset.\",\"available\":true}],\"participantInputs\":[{\"key\":\"mjCaseDetails\",\"question\":\"For the marijuana case: the county, court, docket number, the charge, and when it was decided?\",\"requirement\":\"required\"},{\"key\":\"mjChargeCategory\",\"question\":\"Was the charge unlawful possession of marijuana, or criminal possession of marijuana in the fifth degree, or something else?\",\"requirement\":\"required\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked because only those two categories are covered and anything else is outside the remedy.\"},{\"key\":\"mjMixedCase\",\"question\":\"Did the same case include any charge that was not about marijuana?\",\"requirement\":\"required\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked because a mixed case is outside the remedy.\"},{\"key\":\"mjWantsDestructionRequest\",\"question\":\"Do you want to ask the court where you were convicted to destroy the records of this case? Expungement already happened on its own; destruction only happens if you ask for it.\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked so the destruction request is prepared only where the participant wants it. It is the gate on the whole second stage.\"},{\"key\":\"mjExpungementAlreadyHappened\",\"question\":\"After checking your own record, can you confirm the marijuana case has already been expunged?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only before the destruction request is prepared, because CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i) lets only the individual whose case has been expunged make the request. The participant confirms it from their own record; LegalEase does not certify that the expungement occurred.\"},{\"key\":\"mjRequesterName\",\"question\":\"What is your full legal name, and any other name the case was under?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only for the destruction request, which must identify the person whose case was expunged.\"},{\"key\":\"mjRequesterDateOfBirth\",\"question\":\"What is your date of birth?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only for the destruction request, because it is how the court system locates the record.\"},{\"key\":\"mjRequesterSigner\",\"question\":\"Will you sign the request yourself, or is someone acting as your designated agent?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only for the destruction request. CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i) allows the individual or their designated agent to make it, and the signature block has to match.\"},{\"key\":\"mjReturnContact\",\"question\":\"What postal address, phone number or email should the court use to reply to you?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only for the destruction request, so any reply reaches the participant.\"},{\"key\":\"mjOtherCourtsOfConviction\",\"question\":\"Do you have eligible marihuana convictions in more than one New York court? If so, which courts?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only for the destruction request. One application goes to each court of conviction, so a person with convictions in two courts needs two applications.\"},{\"key\":\"mjSubmissionMethod\",\"question\":\"How do you want to submit the application — through the court's Electronic Document Delivery System, by first-class mail, or in person at the courthouse?\",\"requirement\":\"conditional\",\"conditionDescription\":\"Asked only for the destruction request, because the answer decides how the applicant proves they were the defendant: photo identification in person, or notarization for EDDS, mail or attorney submission.\"}],\"legalDesignDecision\":{\"status\":\"legal_design_approved_with_limitations\",\"rationale\":\"One relief mechanism under CPL § 160.50(5), normalized as a sequential composition rather than as pure guidance. The route was carrying a participant-signed official form inside a no-filing guidance classification: component ny_mrta_marijuana-destruction-request-instructions-2 declared official_pdf_fill and MRTA-DESTRUCTION-REQUEST on a track whose own strategy was process_guidance, and the manual-completion item for it was the participant signing and sending it. A participant-signed, participant-submitted official instrument cannot sit inside a route classified as having no participant filing. Reading CPL § 160.50 at the New York State Senate's published-statutes channel on 2026-08-07 settles the structure and corrects the framing the review left open. Paragraph (5)(a) vacates, dismisses and expunges by operation of law. Paragraph (5)(b)(i) then provides that upon the written request of the individual whose case has been expunged, or their designated agent, such records shall be destroyed. Destruction is therefore not automatic, and the request is not a way to confirm or chase relief the State already gave: it is the sole statutory trigger for a distinct legal effect. Paragraph (5)(b)(ii) places that request inside the same statutory architecture in the statute's own words, describing what the subdivision requires as automatic vacatur, dismissal and expungement including record destruction if requested. That makes this a composed relief mechanism and not a supporting action. The statute creates two legally distinct stages with different actors: at the first the chief administrator of the courts notifies the record holders and the participant does nothing, and at the second the individual or their designated agent makes a written request and the holders destroy. The conditional relationship is legally meaningful, because only the individual whose case has been expunged may make the request, so the second unit presupposes the first. And the request is not a correction or follow-up for a failed automatic process: the statute supplies a separate route for that at § 160.50(5)(b)(ii)(B). A supporting_action node would have had to assert that the automatic process did not occur, which is not this route's premise and which the design must not claim. Both units seek relief under the same subdivision on the same record, so no second relief track is created and no second remedy is offered for relief the State already granted. The automatic stage stays guidance and stays first, no participant filing is generated for it, and the destruction request is produced only where the participant asks for it. What remains open is the Marihuana Regulation and Taxation Act chapter-law citation and the current content of § 160.50(3)(k), the revision and official publication of the retained application, and the procedure around the request.\",\"limitations\":[{\"classification\":\"legal_design_blocker\",\"statement\":\"Pin the MRTA statutory citations\",\"undeterminedElement\":\"governing_mechanism\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"LIMITATIONS, CLASSIFIED\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Pin the MRTA statutory citations | Legal-design blocker\"}},{\"classification\":\"packet_instruction\",\"statement\":\"Expunged marijuana convictions do not count toward the two-conviction limit under CPL 160.59. That is a genuinely valuable rule and should be encoded, because it can move a participant from ineligible to eligible for a discretionary petition.\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"TRACK 3, Interaction with Track 7\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Expunged marijuana convictions do not count toward the two-conviction limit under CPL 160.59. That is a genuinely valuable rule and should be encoded, because it can move a participant from ineligible to eligible for a discretionary petition.\"}},{\"classification\":\"scope_restriction\",\"statement\":\"Sealing is not expungement except for marijuana\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"LIMITATIONS, CLASSIFIED\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Sealing is not expungement except for marijuana | Scope restriction\"}},{\"classification\":\"scope_restriction\",\"statement\":\"Sealed records remain a predicate for sentence enhancement\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"LIMITATIONS, CLASSIFIED\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Sealed records remain a predicate for sentence enhancement | Scope restriction\"}},{\"classification\":\"scope_restriction\",\"statement\":\"Enumerated access exceptions, including vulnerable-population checks, NYSED, transportation network companies, police and peace officer employment, and firearms licensing\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"LIMITATIONS, CLASSIFIED\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Enumerated access exceptions, including vulnerable-population checks, NYSED, transportation network companies, police and peace officer employment, and firearms licensing | Scope restriction\"}},{\"classification\":\"scope_restriction\",\"statement\":\"No reach to federal or out-of-state records, and no immigration effect\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"LIMITATIONS, CLASSIFIED\",\"sourceStatement\":\"No reach to federal or out-of-state records, and no immigration effect | Scope restriction\"}},{\"classification\":\"packet_instruction\",\"statement\":\"Never state that a record is already sealed; verify through DCJS\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"explicit_state_addendum\",\"sourceFile\":\"NY__LEGAL-REVIEW__STATEWIDE__new-york-record-clearing-legal-review__ASOF-2026-08-01__EN.md\",\"sourceHeading\":\"LIMITATIONS, CLASSIFIED\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Never state that a record is already sealed; verify through DCJS | Packet instruction\"}},{\"classification\":\"packet_instruction\",\"statement\":\"Destruction is not automatic. Records of an expunged marijuana conviction are destroyed only upon the written request of the individual whose case has been expunged, or their designated agent. Never tell a participant that their marijuana records have been destroyed, and never present the request as confirming or chasing relief that failed.\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"mechanical_translation\",\"sourceFile\":\"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/CPL/160.50\",\"sourceHeading\":\"CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i), duties of certain state officials and law enforcement agencies\",\"sourceStatement\":\"Upon the written request of the individual whose case has been expunged or their designated agent, such records shall be destroyed.\",\"normalizerInferred\":true}},{\"classification\":\"post_generation_handoff\",\"statement\":\"After the participant signs and submits the destruction request to the court of conviction, the court and the record holders act. The court acknowledges the application and forwards it to the prosecutor, the law enforcement agencies involved and, where the person was fingerprinted, the Division of Criminal Justice Services, which writes to confirm destruction; and under CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i) the chief administrator of the courts is the body that notifies the record holders of the underlying expungement. The participant notifies no agency, serves nobody and delivers no certified copies, and LegalEase does not submit the application.\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"mechanical_translation\",\"sourceFile\":\"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/CPL/160.50\",\"sourceHeading\":\"CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(i), notification and marking\",\"sourceStatement\":\"the chief administrator of the courts shall promptly notify the commissioner of the division of criminal justice services and the heads of all appropriate police departments, district attorney's offices and other law enforcement agencies of all convictions that have been vacated and dismissed pursuant to paragraph (a) of this subdivision\",\"normalizerInferred\":true}},{\"classification\":\"packet_instruction\",\"statement\":\"Where court records cannot be located, or the vacatur, dismissal and expungement has not been reflected, the route is CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(ii)(B) and not the destruction request: the person or their attorney presents a Division of Criminal Justice Services fingerprint record, a copy of a court disposition record or other relevant court record to an appropriate court employee, and the chief administrator of the courts or their designee shall assure completion promptly and in any event within thirty days.\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"mechanical_translation\",\"sourceFile\":\"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/CPL/160.50\",\"sourceHeading\":\"CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(ii)(B), records not located or not updated\",\"sourceStatement\":\"where court records relevant to such matter cannot be located or have been destroyed, and a person or the person's attorney presents to an appropriate court employee a fingerprint record of the New York state division of criminal justice services, or a copy of a court disposition record or other relevant court record ... then promptly, and in any event within thirty days after such notice to such court employee, the chief administrator of the courts or his or her designee shall assure that such vacatur, dismissal, and expungement, including record destruction if requested, have been completed\",\"normalizerInferred\":true}},{\"classification\":\"self_help_boundary\",\"statement\":\"Destruction is irreversible and is not the same as expungement. An expunged conviction is already excluded from a Division of Criminal Justice Services criminal history report, and the statute itself contemplates that a person whose court records have been destroyed may later need to present a fingerprint record or a disposition record to have the relief assured. Say that plainly before the participant signs. LegalEase prepares the request the participant asks for; it does not advise whether to ask for destruction.\",\"provenance\":{\"classificationBasis\":\"mechanical_translation\",\"sourceFile\":\"https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/laws/CPL/160.50\",\"sourceHeading\":\"CPL § 160.50(5)(b)(ii)(A)(1) and (B), consequences of destruction\",\"sourceStatement\":\"when the division of criminal justice services conducts a search of its criminal history records ... and returns a report thereon, all references to a conviction for an offense described in paragraph (k) of subdivision three of this section shall be excluded from such report\",\"normalizerInferred\":true}}]}}";
